{=============================================================================
  Plate heat exchangers: single-phase Nusselt numbers and two-phase boiling
  heat-transfer coefficients (function library)
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0096 (function library, kind = function)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Correlations for single-phase flow and for flow boiling inside the
  corrugated channels of a chevron-style plate-and-frame heat exchanger.

  Four single-phase correlations return the Nusselt number of the channel
  (Nu_plate_Kumar, Nu_plate_Martin, Nu_plate_Muley_Manglik, Nu_plate_Khan_Khan
  of ht) and five flow-boiling correlations return the heat-transfer
  coefficient of the channel (h_boiling_Amalfi, h_boiling_Lee_Kang_Kim,
  h_boiling_Han_Lee_Kim, h_boiling_Huang_Sheer, h_boiling_Yan_Lin of ht).

  Two more EES functions, fd_Martin_1999 and fd_Martin_VDI, are the two
  friction-factor correlations of Martin that Nu_plate_Martin calls inside ht
  (they come from the companion library fluids); they are translated here
  because the Nu correlation cannot be evaluated without them.

  Every boiling correlation is written for a given heat flux q [W/m^2]: it is
  a local correlation of a plate evaporator, not of the whole exchanger. All
  the fluid properties are arguments of the functions: the calling model
  evaluates them (rule 5 of sources/ht/README.md, section 7).

  The demonstration program at the end calls every function, once with the
  inputs of the ht doctest of the corresponding correlation ("A" cases) and
  once with a second, uniform input set ("B" cases: a 60 degree brazed-plate
  R134a evaporator). The ht values of both sets are tabulated in the README
  (verification table).

  No dispatcher of the family had to be dropped: ht exports nine functions
  and all nine are translated.

  Inputs : Re [-], Pr [-], chevron_angle [degrees], plate_enlargement_factor [-],
           quality x [-], Dh or D_eq [m], mass flow rate m [kg/s] per channel,
           channel flow area A_channel_flow [m^2], wavelength [m], rhol, rhog
           [kg/m^3], mul, mug [Pa-s], kl [W/m-K], Hvap [J/kg], sigma [N/m],
           Cpl [J/kg-K], q [W/m^2], Tsat [K], variant and angle flags [-]
  Outputs: Nu [-] (single-phase), h [W/m^2-K] (flow boiling), fd [-]
  Units  : SI - lengths in m, degrees for the chevron and contact angles
  Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/conv_plate.py and ht/boiling_plate.py, version
           1.2.0, commit 85e0ee6, MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ;
           correlations by the authors cited in each function block - see
           README.md
  Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0
           (README.md, "Verification")
 =============================================================================}

FUNCTION fd_Martin_1999(Re, chevron_angle)
{FD_MARTIN_1999 - Darcy friction factor of single-phase flow in the
corrugated channel of a chevron plate heat exchanger, correlation of 1999.

  1/SQRT(f_f) = COS(beta)/SQRT(0.045*TAN(beta) + 0.09*SIN(beta) + f0/COS(beta))
               + (1 - COS(beta))/SQRT(3.8*f1)
  f0 = 16/Re for Re < 2000, (1.56*LN(Re) - 3)^-2 for Re >= 2000
  f1 = 149/Re + 0.9625 for Re < 2000, 9.75*Re^-0.289 for Re >= 2000
  fd = 4*f_f (the correlation is written on the Fanning factor)

  COS, SIN and TAN take degrees, as in EES and CoolSolve (the argument of the
  Python code is converted to radians before the call).
  The regime switch at Re = 2000 is discontinuous in the correlation (see the
  notes of the original) and is written with two sequential IF blocks.
Validity: Re from 200 to 10000, chevron_angle from 0 to 80 degrees (as in
  fluids).  Re is on the hydraulic diameter of the channel [-].
Original: Martin, Holger, "Economic optimization of compact heat exchangers",
  EF-Conference on Compact Heat Exchangers and Enhancement Technology for the
  Process Industries, Banff, Canada, July 18-23, 1999 (also Shah, R. K. and
  D. P. Sekulic, Fundamentals of Heat Exchanger Design, Wiley, 2002); the same
  model is first published in Martin, H., "A Theoretical Approach to Predict
  the Performance of Chevron-Type Plate Heat Exchangers", Chem. Eng. Process.
  Process Intensification 35(4): 301-310, 1996.
Taken from: fd_Martin_1999 = friction_plate_Martin_1999 of the companion
  library fluids (C. Bell), 1.3.1, called by ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6) in
  ht/conv_plate.py, function Nu_plate_Martin, MIT License -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/fluids}
  f0 = 16/Re
  f1 = 149/Re + 0.9625
  IF Re >= 2000 THEN
    f0 = (1.56*LN(Re) - 3)^-2
    f1 = 9.75*Re^-0.289
  ENDIF
  rhs = COS(chevron_angle)/SQRT(0.045*TAN(chevron_angle) + 0.09*SIN(chevron_angle) + f0/COS(chevron_angle))
  rhs = rhs + (1 - COS(chevron_angle))/SQRT(3.8*f1)
  fd_Martin_1999 = 4*rhs^-2
END

FUNCTION fd_Martin_VDI(Re, chevron_angle)
{FD_MARTIN_VDI - Darcy friction factor of single-phase flow in the corrugated
channel of a chevron plate heat exchanger, the revision of 1999 published in
the VDI Heat Atlas (slightly higher than the 1999 correlation).

  1/SQRT(fd) = COS(beta)/SQRT(0.18*TAN(beta) + 0.36*SIN(beta) + f0/COS(beta))
              + (1 - COS(beta))/SQRT(3.8*f1)
  f0 = 64/Re for Re < 2000, (1.8*LOG10(Re) - 1.5)^-2 for Re >= 2000
  f1 = 597/Re + 3.85 for Re < 2000, 39*Re^-0.289 for Re >= 2000
  a = 3.8, b = 0.18, c = 0.36 (the three adjustable constants of the model)

  COS, SIN and TAN take degrees, as in EES and CoolSolve.
  The regime switch at Re = 2000 is discontinuous in the correlation and is
  written with two sequential IF blocks.
Validity: Re from 200 to 10000, chevron_angle from 0 to 80 degrees (as in
  fluids).  Re is on the hydraulic diameter of the channel [-].
Original: V. D. I. (ed.), VDI Heat Atlas, 2nd edition, Springer, Berlin / New
  York, 2010, section G1 (plate heat exchangers).
Taken from: fd_Martin_VDI = friction_plate_Martin_VDI of the companion
  library fluids (C. Bell), 1.3.1, called by ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6) in
  ht/conv_plate.py, function Nu_plate_Martin, MIT License -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/fluids}
  a = 3.8
  b = 0.18
  c = 0.36
  f0 = 64/Re
  f1 = 597/Re + 3.85
  IF Re >= 2000 THEN
    f0 = (1.8*LOG10(Re) - 1.5)^-2
    f1 = 39*Re^-0.289
  ENDIF
  rhs = COS(chevron_angle)/SQRT(b*TAN(chevron_angle) + c*SIN(chevron_angle) + f0/COS(chevron_angle))
  rhs = rhs + (1 - COS(chevron_angle))/SQRT(a*f1)
  fd_Martin_VDI = rhs^-2
END

FUNCTION Nu_Kumar(Re, Pr, chevron_angle, mu, mu_wall)
{NU_KUMAR - Nusselt number of single-phase flow in the channel of a
**well-designed** Chevron-style plate heat exchanger; the coefficients C1 and m
are read from tables, first on the chevron angle, then on Re, and a wall
viscosity correction can be applied.

  Nu = C1*Re^m*Pr^0.33*(mu/mu_wall)^0.17

  The 5 chevron-angle rows of the 3 columns of the original (the column is
  selected by Re, the largest column being used above its limit) are, in the
  order chevron_angle <= 30, <= 45, <= 50, <= 60 and > 60 degrees:
    (C1, m, Re upper limit) = (0.718, 0.349, 10), (0.348, 0.663, 10),
                              (0.348, 0.663, 100)
                            = (0.718, 0.349, 10), (0.400, 0.598, 100),
                              (0.300, 0.663, 300)
                            = (0.630, 0.333, 20), (0.291, 0.591, 300),
                              (0.130, 0.732, 400)
                            = (0.562, 0.326, 20), (0.306, 0.529, 400),
                              (0.108, 0.703, 500)
                            = (0.562, 0.326, 20), (0.331, 0.503, 400),
                              (0.087, 0.718, 500)

  The data were developed by APV International Limited; the fit of the graph of
  the original is by the author of [2]. "The author of [1] states clearly this
  correlation is 'applicable only to well designed Chevron PHEs'" (as in the
  original). The viscosity correction is only applied when both viscosities
  are given in ht; EES has no optional argument, so mu [Pa-s] and mu_wall
  [Pa-s] are mandatory and the caller passes mu = mu_wall for the form without
  the correction. Passing a chevron angle above 65 degrees gives the last row,
  as in ht. The coefficients change from one row/column to the next, so the
  result has the small discontinuities of the original.
Validity: Re from 0.1 to 10000, chevron_angle from 30 to 65 degrees (as in ht).
Original: Kumar, H., "The plate heat exchanger: construction and design", in
  First U.K. National Conference on Heat Transfer, University of Leeds, 3-5
  July 1984, IChemE Symposium Series 86: 1275-1288, 1984; the curve fit is
  Ayub, Z. H., "Plate Heat Exchanger Literature Survey and New Heat Transfer
  and Pressure Drop Correlations for Refrigerant Evaporators", Heat Transfer
  Engineering 24(5): 3-16, 2003, doi:10.1080/01457630304056.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_plate.py, Nu_plate_Kumar,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  "row of the coefficient tables, selected by the chevron angle"
  i_angle = 1
  IF chevron_angle > 30 THEN
    i_angle = 2
  ENDIF
  IF chevron_angle > 45 THEN
    i_angle = 3
  ENDIF
  IF chevron_angle > 50 THEN
    i_angle = 4
  ENDIF
  IF chevron_angle > 60 THEN
    i_angle = 5
  ENDIF
  "row 1: chevron_angle <= 30 degrees - Reynolds-number limit, C1 and m of the three columns"
  Re_lim_1 = 10
  Re_lim_2 = 10
  Re_lim_3 = 100
  C1_1 = 0.718
  C1_2 = 0.348
  C1_3 = 0.348
  m_1 = 0.349
  m_2 = 0.663
  m_3 = 0.663
  IF i_angle = 2 THEN
    "row 2: chevron_angle <= 45 degrees - Reynolds-number limit, C1 and m of the three columns"
    Re_lim_1 = 10
    Re_lim_2 = 100
    Re_lim_3 = 300
    C1_1 = 0.718
    C1_2 = 0.4
    C1_3 = 0.3
    m_1 = 0.349
    m_2 = 0.598
    m_3 = 0.663
  ENDIF
  IF i_angle = 3 THEN
    "row 3: chevron_angle <= 50 degrees - Reynolds-number limit, C1 and m of the three columns"
    Re_lim_1 = 20
    Re_lim_2 = 300
    Re_lim_3 = 400
    C1_1 = 0.63
    C1_2 = 0.291
    C1_3 = 0.13
    m_1 = 0.333
    m_2 = 0.591
    m_3 = 0.732
  ENDIF
  IF i_angle = 4 THEN
    "row 4: chevron_angle <= 60 degrees - Reynolds-number limit, C1 and m of the three columns"
    Re_lim_1 = 20
    Re_lim_2 = 400
    Re_lim_3 = 500
    C1_1 = 0.562
    C1_2 = 0.306
    C1_3 = 0.108
    m_1 = 0.326
    m_2 = 0.529
    m_3 = 0.703
  ENDIF
  IF i_angle = 5 THEN
    "row 5: chevron_angle above 60 degrees - Reynolds-number limit, C1 and m of the three columns"
    Re_lim_1 = 20
    Re_lim_2 = 400
    Re_lim_3 = 500
    C1_1 = 0.562
    C1_2 = 0.331
    C1_3 = 0.087
    m_1 = 0.326
    m_2 = 0.503
    m_3 = 0.718
  ENDIF

  "column of the row: the first of its three Reynolds-number limits the number does not exceed"
  i_Re = 1
  IF Re > Re_lim_1 THEN
    i_Re = 2
  ENDIF
  IF Re > Re_lim_2 THEN
    i_Re = 3
  ENDIF
  C1 = C1_1
  m = m_1
  IF i_Re = 2 THEN
    C1 = C1_2
    m = m_2
  ENDIF
  IF i_Re = 3 THEN
    C1 = C1_3
    m = m_3
  ENDIF
  Nu_Kumar = C1*Re^m*Pr^0.33*(mu/mu_wall)^0.17
END

FUNCTION Nu_Martin(Re, Pr, chevron_angle, variant)
{NU_MARTIN - Nusselt number of single-phase flow in the channel of a
Chevron-style plate heat exchanger.

  Nu = 0.122*Pr^(1/3)*[fd*Re^2*SIN(2*beta)]^0.374

  fd is the Darcy friction factor computed with the correlation of Martin
  (1999) or with his later formulation of the VDI Heat Atlas, chosen by the
  flag variant (0 = 1999, 1 = VDI); they are the two EES functions
  fd_Martin_1999 and fd_Martin_VDI of this file, as in ht.
  SIN takes degrees in EES and CoolSolve: the argument of the Python code,
  2*radians(chevron_angle), is therefore written SIN(2*chevron_angle).
Validity: experimental data from Re = 200 to 10000, chevron angles from 0 to
  80 degrees (as in ht).  Re is on the hydraulic diameter of the channel [-].
Original: Martin, Holger, "A Theoretical Approach to Predict the Performance
  of Chevron-Type Plate Heat Exchangers", Chemical Engineering and Processing:
  Process Intensification 35(4): 301-310, 1996, doi:10.1016/0255-2701(95)04129-X;
  friction factor and economic optimization, Martin, Holger, "Economic
  optimization of compact heat exchangers", EF-Conference on Compact Heat
  Exchangers and Enhancement Technology for the Process Industries, Banff,
  Canada, July 18-23, 1999; VDI Heat Atlas, 2nd edition, Springer, 2010.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_plate.py, Nu_plate_Martin,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  IF variant = 0 THEN
    fd = fd_Martin_1999(Re, chevron_angle)
  ENDIF
  IF variant = 1 THEN
    fd = fd_Martin_VDI(Re, chevron_angle)
  ENDIF
  Nu_Martin = 0.122*Pr^(1/3)*(fd*Re^2*SIN(2*chevron_angle))^0.374
END

FUNCTION Nu_Muley_Manglik(Re, Pr, chevron_angle, plate_enlargement_factor)
{NU_MULEY_MANGLIK - Nusselt number of single-phase flow in the channel of a
Chevron-style plate heat exchanger.

  Nu = [0.2668 - 0.006967*beta + 7.244E-5*beta^2]
      *[20.7803 - 50.9372*phi + 41.1585*phi^2 - 10.1507*phi^3]
      *Re^[0.728 + 0.0543*SIN(2*pi*beta/90 + 3.7)]*Pr^(1/3)

  beta = chevron_angle [degrees] and phi = plate_enlargement_factor = the extra
  surface area multiplier as compared to a flat plate caused by the
  corrugations [-].
  "the correlation as presented in the original suffers from a typo, with a
   coefficient of 10.51 instead of 10.15. Several more decimal places were
   published along with the corrected typo in [2]. This has a *very large*
   difference if not implemented" (as in the original): the corrected form is
   used here.
  SIN takes degrees in EES and CoolSolve: the argument of the Python code,
  2*pi*beta/90 + 3.7 rad, is therefore written SIN(4*beta + 3.7*180/pi()).
Validity: Re above 1000, chevron_angle between 30 and 60 degrees, enlargement
  factors from 1 to 1.5 (as in ht); because of its cubic form the
  correlation is not likely to be accurate outside those ranges.
Original: Muley, A. and R. M. Manglik, "Experimental Study of Turbulent Flow
  Heat Transfer and Pressure Drop in a Plate Heat Exchanger With Chevron
  Plates", Journal of Heat Transfer 121(1): 110-117, February 1999,
  doi:10.1115/1.2825923; corrected form in Palm, B. and J. Claesson, "Plate
  Heat Exchangers: Calculation Methods for Single- and Two-Phase Flow
  (Keynote)", Proceedings of the International Conference on Mechanical and
  Metallurgical Technology, ICMM2005: 103-113, 2005, doi:10.1115/ICMM2005-75092.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_plate.py, Nu_plate_Muley_Manglik,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  t1 = 0.2668 - 0.006967*chevron_angle + 7.244E-5*chevron_angle^2
  t2 = 20.7803 - 50.9372*plate_enlargement_factor + 41.1585*plate_enlargement_factor^2 - 10.1507*plate_enlargement_factor^3
  t3 = 0.728 + 0.0543*SIN(4*chevron_angle + 3.7*180/pi())
  Nu_Muley_Manglik = t1*t2*Re^t3*Pr^(1/3)
END

FUNCTION Nu_Khan_Khan(Re, Pr, chevron_angle)
{NU_KHAN_KHAN - Nusselt number of single-phase flow in the channel of a
Chevron-style plate heat exchanger.

  Nu = (0.0161*beta/beta_max + 0.1298)*Re^(0.198*beta/beta_max + 0.6398)*Pr^0.35

  beta_max = 60 degrees.  The viscosity correction power recommended with this
  correlation is the blanket Sieder and Tate (1936) value of 0.14 (as in the
  original); it is not part of the equation and is left to the caller.
Validity: Re from 500 to 2500, chevron_angle between 30 and 60 degrees,
  Prandtl numbers between 3.5 and 6 (as in ht).
Original: Khan, T. S., M. S. Khan, M.-C. Chyu and Z. H. Ayub, "Experimental
  Investigation of Single Phase Convective Heat Transfer Coefficient in a
  Corrugated Plate Heat Exchanger for Multiple Plate Configurations", Applied
  Thermal Engineering 30(8): 1058-1065, 1 June 2010,
  doi:10.1016/j.applthermaleng.2010.01.021.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_plate.py, Nu_plate_Khan_Khan,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  beta_ratio = chevron_angle/60
  Nu_Khan_Khan = (0.0161*beta_ratio + 0.1298)*Re^(0.198*beta_ratio + 0.6398)*Pr^0.35
END

FUNCTION h_Amalfi(m, x, Dh, rhol, rhog, mul, mug, kl, Hvap, sigma, q, A_channel_flow, chevron_angle)
{H_AMALFI - two-phase (flow boiling) heat-transfer coefficient of a liquid and
vapour flowing in a plate and frame heat exchanger; a correlation developed
from a wide range of existing correlations and data sets, which ht presents as
the most accurate currently available.

  For the Bond number Bd < 4 ("tiny channel case"):
    h = 982*(kl/Dh)*(beta/beta_max)^1.101*(G^2*Dh/(rho_h*sigma))^0.315
        *(rhol/rhog)^-0.224*Bo^0.320
  For the Bond number Bd >= 4:
    h = 18.495*(kl/Dh)*(beta/beta_max)^0.135*Re_g^0.135*Re_lo^0.351
        *Bd^0.235*Bo^0.198*(rhol/rhog)^-0.223

  beta_max = 45 degrees; G = m/A_channel_flow is the mass flux of the channel;
  rho_h = 1/(x/rhog + (1-x)/rhol) is the homogeneous holdup density;
  Re_lo = G*Dh/mul and Re_g = G*x*Dh/mug; Bo = q/(G*Hvap) is the boiling
  number and Bd = g*(rhol - rhog)*Dh^2/sigma is the Bond number.
  Note that the exponent of (beta/beta_max) in the second equation is 0.135 in
  the code of ht, which is what this function implements, while the formula
  printed in its docstring gives 0.248; the value 776.078 of the doctest is on
  the Bd < 4 branch and does not discriminate between the two.
  The chevron_angle argument is mandatory here (it has the default value of 45
  degrees in ht) and the switch Bd < 4 is discontinuous by nature (EES IF).
Validity: developed from a 1903-point database; the fluids of the original are
  R134a, ammonia, R236fa, R600a, R290, R1270, R1234yf, R410A, R507A,
  ammonia/water and air/water mixtures, over a wide range of operating
  conditions and plate geometries (as in ht).
Original: Amalfi, R. L., F. Vakili-Farahani and J. R. Thome, "Flow Boiling
  and Frictional Pressure Gradients in Plate Heat Exchangers. Part 2:
  Comparison of Literature Methods to Database and New Prediction Methods",
  International Journal of Refrigeration 61: 185-203, January 2016,
  doi:10.1016/j.ijrefrig.2015.07.009.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_plate.py, h_boiling_Amalfi,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  g_acc = 9.80665    "gravitational acceleration [m/s^2]; the EES constant g is not resolved inside a function body (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  beta_s = chevron_angle/45
  rho_s = rhol/rhog
  G = m/A_channel_flow
  Bd = g_acc*(rhol - rhog)*Dh^2/sigma
  rho_h = 1/(x/rhog + (1 - x)/rhol)
  We_m = G^2*Dh/sigma/rho_h
  Bo = q/(G*Hvap)
  IF Bd < 4 THEN
    Nu_tp = 982*beta_s^1.101*We_m^0.315*Bo^0.320*rho_s^-0.224
  ENDIF
  IF Bd >= 4 THEN
    Re_lo = G*Dh/mul
    Re_g = G*x*Dh/mug
    Nu_tp = 18.495*beta_s^0.135*Re_g^0.135*Re_lo^0.351*Bd^0.235*Bo^0.198*rho_s^-0.223
  ENDIF
  h_Amalfi = kl/Dh*Nu_tp
END

FUNCTION h_Lee_Kang_Kim(m, x, D_eq, rhol, rhog, mul, mug, kl, Hvap, q, A_channel_flow)
{H_LEE_KANG_KIM - two-phase (flow boiling) heat-transfer coefficient of a liquid
and vapour flowing in a plate and frame heat exchanger, as reviewed in [2].

  For Re_g/Re_l < 9:
    h = 98.7*(kl/D_eq)*(Re_g/Re_l)^-0.0848*Bo^-0.0597*Xtt^0.0973
  For Re_g/Re_l >= 9:
    h = 234.9*(kl/D_eq)*(Re_g/Re_l)^-0.576*Bo^-0.275*Xtt^0.66

  G = m/A_channel_flow is the mass flux of the channel, Bo = q/(G*Hvap) is the
  boiling number and Re_g/Re_l = x/(1-x)*mul/mug; the two-phase parameter is
  the Lockhart-Martinelli one of [1] with the powers of the original,
  Xtt = ((1-x)/x)^0.875*(rhog/rhol)^0.5*(mul/mug)^0.125.
  Note that this model depends on the specific heat flux involved. It also
  uses the equivalent diameter, not the hydraulic diameter (as in the
  original).
Validity: developed with mass fluxes from 14.5 to 33.6 kg/m^2/s, heat flux
  from 15 to 30 kW/m^2, qualities from 0.09 to 0.6, 200 < Re < 600,
  2.3 < Re_g/Re_l < 32.1, 0.00019 < Bo < 0.001, 0.028 < Xtt < 0.3; mean
  average deviation of 4.4 % (as in ht).
Original: Lee, E., H. Kang and Y. Kim, "Flow Boiling Heat Transfer and
  Pressure Drop of Water in a Plate Heat Exchanger with Corrugated Channels at
  Low Mass Flux Conditions", International Journal of Heat and Mass Transfer
  77: 37-45, October 2014, doi:10.1016/j.ijheatmasstransfer.2014.05.019;
  review: Amalfi, R. L., F. Vakili-Farahani and J. R. Thome, "Flow Boiling
  and Frictional Pressure Gradients in Plate Heat Exchangers. Part 1: Review
  and Experimental Database", International Journal of Refrigeration 61:
  166-184, January 2016, doi:10.1016/j.ijrefrig.2015.07.010; Xtt form of
  Woldesemayat, M. A. and A. J. Ghajar, "Comparison of Void Fraction
  Correlations for Different Flow Patterns in Horizontal and Upward Inclined
  Pipes", International Journal of Multiphase Flow 33(4): 347-370, 2007.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_plate.py,
  h_boiling_Lee_Kang_Kim, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  G = m/A_channel_flow
  Bo = q/(G*Hvap)
  Re_ratio = x/(1 - x)*mul/mug
  Xtt = ((1 - x)/x)^0.875*(rhog/rhol)^0.5*(mul/mug)^0.125
  IF Re_ratio < 9 THEN
    h_Lee_Kang_Kim = 98.7*kl/D_eq*Re_ratio^-0.0848*Bo^-0.0597*Xtt^0.0973
  ENDIF
  IF Re_ratio >= 9 THEN
    h_Lee_Kang_Kim = 234.9*kl/D_eq*Re_ratio^-0.576*Bo^-0.275*Xtt^0.66
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION h_Han_Lee_Kim(m, x, Dh, rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Hvap, Cpl, q, A_channel_flow, wavelength, chevron_angle)
{H_HAN_LEE_KIM - two-phase (flow boiling) heat-transfer coefficient of a liquid
and vapour flowing in a plate and frame heat exchanger, developed from
experiments with three plate exchangers and the working fluids R410A and R22.

  h = Ge_1*(kl/Dh)*Re_eq^Ge_2*Pr^0.4*Bo_eq^0.3
  Ge_1 = 2.81*(lambda/Dh)^-0.041*(pi/2 - beta)^-2.83
  Ge_2 = 0.746*(lambda/Dh)^-0.082*(pi/2 - beta)^0.61
  Re_eq = G_eq*Dh/mul,  Bo_eq = q/(G_eq*Hvap),  Pr = Cpl*mul/kl
  G_eq = (m/A_channel_flow)*[1 - x + x*(rhol/rhog)^0.5]

  lambda is the wavelength (pitch) of the corrugations, the flow area is twice
  the corrugation amplitude times the width of the plate and the mass flow is
  that of one channel. The angle beta is the chevron angle converted to
  radians: "Radians is used in degrees, and the formulas are for the
  inclination angle not the chevron angle (it is converted internally)" (as in
  the original). Note that this model depends on the specific heat flux
  involved.
Validity: developed with three plate heat exchangers with angles of 45, 35 and
  20 degrees, mass fluxes from 13 to 34 kg/m^2/s, evaporating temperatures of
  5, 10 and 15 degrees, vapour qualities from 0.15 to 0.9 and heat fluxes of
  2.5 to 8.5 kW/m^2 (as in ht).
Original: Han, D.-H., K.-J. Lee and Y.-H. Kim, "Experiments on the
  Characteristics of Evaporation of R410A in Brazed Plate Heat Exchangers
  with Different Geometric Configurations", Applied Thermal Engineering 23(10):
  1209-1225, July 2003, doi:10.1016/S1359-4311(03)00061-9.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_plate.py,
  h_boiling_Han_Lee_Kim, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  beta = chevron_angle*pi()/180    "chevron angle [rad]"
  G = m/A_channel_flow
  G_eq = G*((1 - x) + x*(rhol/rhog)^0.5)
  Re_eq = G_eq*Dh/mul
  Bo_eq = q/(G_eq*Hvap)
  Pr = Cpl*mul/kl
  Ge1 = 2.81*(wavelength/Dh)^-0.041*beta^-2.83
  Ge2 = 0.746*(wavelength/Dh)^-0.082*beta^0.61
  h_Han_Lee_Kim = Ge1*kl/Dh*Re_eq^Ge2*Pr^0.4*Bo_eq^0.3
END

FUNCTION h_Huang_Sheer(rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Hvap, sigma, Cpl, q, Tsat, angle)
{H_HUANG_SHEER - two-phase (flow boiling) heat-transfer coefficient of a liquid
and vapour flowing in a plate and frame heat exchanger; a correlation that
depends on the properties of the fluid and not on the geometry of the
exchanger.

  h = 1.87E-3*(kl/d_o)*(q*d_o/(kl*Tsat))^0.56*(Hvap*d_o^2/alpha_l^2)^0.31*Pr_l^0.33
  d_o = 0.0146*theta*[2*sigma/(g*(rhol - rhog))]^0.5   with theta = 35 degrees
  Pr_l = Cpl*mul/kl  and  alpha_l = kl/(rhol*Cpl) [m^2/s]

  d_o is the bubble diameter, the angle argument being the contact angle of
  the bubbles with the wall (assumed to be 35 degrees for the refrigerants of
  the development of the correlation); it is used in degrees, as in the code of
  ht. "As first published in [1] a power of two was missing in the
  correlation for bubble diameter in the dimensionless group with a power of
  0.31. That made the correlation non-dimensional" (as in the original).
  Tsat is the saturation temperature in K, as in ht. Note that this model
  depends on the specific heat flux involved and on the saturation
  temperature.
Validity: developed with 222 data points for R134a and R507A (only two for
  ammonia and R12), chevron angles from 28 to 60 degrees, heat fluxes from
  1.85 to 10.75 kW/m^2, mass fluxes from 5.6 to 52.25 kg/m^2/s, qualities
  from 0.21 to 0.95 and saturation temperatures of 1.9 to 13.04 degrees
  Celsius (as in ht). "The inclusion of the saturation temperature makes this
  correlation have limited predictive power for other fluids whose saturation
  tempratures might be much higher or lower than those used in the
  development of the correlation. For this reason it should be regarded with
  caution" (as in the original).
Original: Huang, J., T. J. Sheer and M. Bailey-McEwan, "Heat Transfer and
  Pressure Drop in Plate Heat Exchanger Refrigerant Evaporators", International
  Journal of Refrigeration 35(2): 325-335, March 2012,
  doi:10.1016/j.ijrefrig.2011.11.002.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_plate.py,
  h_boiling_Huang_Sheer, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  g_acc = 9.80665    "gravitational acceleration [m/s^2]; the EES constant g is not resolved inside a function body (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  d_o = 0.0146*angle*(2*sigma/(g_acc*(rhol - rhog)))^0.5
  Prl = Cpl*mul/kl
  alpha_l = kl/(rhol*Cpl)
  h_Huang_Sheer = 1.87E-3*(kl/d_o)*(q*d_o/(kl*Tsat))^0.56*(Hvap*d_o^2/alpha_l^2)^0.31*Prl^0.33
END

FUNCTION h_Yan_Lin(m, x, Dh, rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Hvap, Cpl, q, A_channel_flow)
{H_YAN_LIN - two-phase (flow boiling) heat-transfer coefficient of a liquid and
vapour flowing in a plate and frame heat exchanger.

  h = 1.926*(kl/Dh)*Re_eq*Pr_l^(1/3)*Bo_eq^0.3*Re^-0.5
  Re_eq = G_eq*Dh/mul,  Bo_eq = q/(G_eq*Hvap),  Pr_l = Cpl*mul/kl
  G_eq = (m/A_channel_flow)*[1 - x + x*(rhol/rhog)^0.5],  Re = (m/A_channel_flow)*Dh/mul

  The factor Re^-0.5, written in the equation of the original as well, is kept
  as it is: the code of ht notes for it that it is "not actually specified
  clearly but it is in another paper by them" (as in the original).
Validity: developed with R134a as the refrigerant in a plate heat exchanger
  with 2 channels, chevron angle 60 degrees, quality from 0.1 to 0.8, heat
  flux 11 to 15 kW/m^2 and mass fluxes of 55 and 70 kg/m^2/s; claimed to be
  valid for 2000 < Re_eq < 10000 (as in ht).
Original: Yan, Y.-Y. and T.-F. Lin, "Evaporation Heat Transfer and Pressure
  Drop of Refrigerant R-134a in a Plate Heat Exchanger", Journal of Heat
  Transfer 121(1): 118-127, February 1999, doi:10.1115/1.2825924.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_plate.py, h_boiling_Yan_Lin,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  G = m/A_channel_flow
  G_eq = G*((1 - x) + x*(rhol/rhog)^0.5)
  Re_eq = G_eq*Dh/mul
  Re = G*Dh/mul
  Bo_eq = q/(G_eq*Hvap)
  Pr_l = Cpl*mul/kl
  h_Yan_Lin = 1.926*(kl/Dh)*Re_eq*Pr_l^(1/3)*Bo_eq^0.3*Re^-0.5
END

"!Demonstration program: every correlation is called twice"
"It is ignored by the future $INCLUDE library:plate_hx_heat_transfer (CS-FEAT-IMPORT)."
"Case A: the input set of the ht doctest of each correlation (published values)."
"Case B: one second, uniform input set: a 60 degree brazed-plate R134a evaporator."
"Both cases are tabulated against ht in the README (verification table)."

"!Case A - input set"
Re_A = 2000
Pr_A = 0.7
chevron_angle_A = 30
F_A = 1.18
m_A = 3E-5
x_A = 0.4
Dh_A = 1.72E-3
D_eq_A = 2E-3
rhol_A = 567
rhog_A = 18.09
mul_A = 156E-6
mug_A = 7.11E-6
kl_A = 0.086
Hvap_A = 9E5
sigma_A = 0.02
Cpl_A = 2200
q_A = 1E5
A_ch_A = 3E-4
wavelength_A = 3.7E-3
Tsat_A = 279.15
mu_A = 1E-3
mu_wall_A = 8E-4

"fd_Martin_1999 - turbulent branch of the correlation (Re = 20000)"
fd_Martin_1999_A = fd_Martin_1999(20000, 45)
"Nu_Kumar - without and with the wall viscosity correction (mu = mu_wall)"
Nu_Kumar_A = Nu_Kumar(Re_A, Pr_A, chevron_angle_A, 1, 1)
Nu_Kumar_visc_A = Nu_Kumar(Re_A, Pr_A, chevron_angle_A, mu_A, mu_wall_A)
"Nu_Martin - friction factor of 1999 (variant = 0) and of the VDI Heat Atlas (variant = 1)"
Nu_Martin_A = Nu_Martin(2000, 0.7, 45, 0)
Nu_Martin_VDI_A = Nu_Martin(2000, 0.7, 45, 1)
"Nu_Muley_Manglik - chevron angle of 45 degrees, enlargement factor 1.18"
Nu_Muley_Manglik_A = Nu_Muley_Manglik(Re_A, Pr_A, 45, F_A)
"Nu_Khan_Khan - chevron angle of 30 degrees"
Nu_Khan_Khan_A = Nu_Khan_Khan(1000, 4.5, 30)
"h_Amalfi - Bond number < 4 branch (small channel)"
h_Amalfi_A = h_Amalfi(m_A, x_A, Dh_A, rhol_A, rhog_A, mul_A, mug_A, kl_A, Hvap_A, sigma_A, q_A, A_ch_A, 45)
"h_Lee_Kang_Kim - equivalent diameter of 2 mm"
h_Lee_Kang_Kim_A = h_Lee_Kang_Kim(m_A, x_A, D_eq_A, rhol_A, rhog_A, mul_A, 9E-6, kl_A, Hvap_A, q_A, A_ch_A)
"h_Han_Lee_Kim - wavelength of 3.7 mm, chevron angle of 45 degrees"
h_Han_Lee_Kim_A = h_Han_Lee_Kim(m_A, x_A, 2E-3, rhol_A, rhog_A, mul_A, kl_A, Hvap_A, Cpl_A, q_A, A_ch_A, wavelength_A, 45)
"h_Huang_Sheer - saturation temperature of 279.15 K, contact angle of 35 degrees, q = 10 kW/m^2"
h_Huang_Sheer_A = h_Huang_Sheer(rhol_A, rhog_A, mul_A, kl_A, Hvap_A, sigma_A, Cpl_A, 1E4, Tsat_A, 35)
"h_Yan_Lin - hydraulic diameter of 2 mm"
h_Yan_Lin_A = h_Yan_Lin(m_A, x_A, 2E-3, rhol_A, rhog_A, mul_A, kl_A, Hvap_A, Cpl_A, q_A, A_ch_A)

"!Case B - second, uniform input set"
"60 degree brazed-plate R134a evaporator, approximate fluid properties at 10 degrees Celsius"
Re_B = 6000
Pr_B = 4.5
chevron_angle_B = 60
F_B = 1.25
m_B = 8E-3
x_B = 0.35
Dh_B = 3E-3
D_eq_B = 7.4E-3
rhol_B = 1206
rhog_B = 41.9
mul_B = 2.6E-4
mug_B = 1.35E-5
kl_B = 0.0835
Hvap_B = 201300
sigma_B = 0.0143
Cpl_B = 1420
q_B = 5E4
A_ch_B = 4E-4
wavelength_B = 3.7E-3
Tsat_B = 283.15
mu_B = 2.6E-4
mu_wall_B = 2E-4

"fd_Martin_1999 and fd_Martin_VDI at the chevron angle of 60 degrees"
fd_Martin_1999_B = fd_Martin_1999(Re_B, chevron_angle_B)
fd_Martin_VDI_B = fd_Martin_VDI(Re_B, chevron_angle_B)
"Nu_Kumar - wall viscosity correction, chevron angle of 60 degrees"
Nu_Kumar_B = Nu_Kumar(Re_B, Pr_B, chevron_angle_B, mu_B, mu_wall_B)
"Nu_Martin - both friction factors"
Nu_Martin_B = Nu_Martin(Re_B, Pr_B, chevron_angle_B, 0)
Nu_Martin_VDI_B = Nu_Martin(Re_B, Pr_B, chevron_angle_B, 1)
"Nu_Muley_Manglik"
Nu_Muley_Manglik_B = Nu_Muley_Manglik(Re_B, Pr_B, chevron_angle_B, F_B)
"Nu_Khan_Khan"
Nu_Khan_Khan_B = Nu_Khan_Khan(Re_B, Pr_B, chevron_angle_B)
"h_Amalfi - Bond number >= 4 branch (mass flux of 20 kg/m^2/s)"
h_Amalfi_B = h_Amalfi(m_B, x_B, Dh_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, mug_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, q_B, A_ch_B, chevron_angle_B)
"h_Lee_Kang_Kim - Re_g/Re_l >= 9 branch"
h_Lee_Kang_Kim_B = h_Lee_Kang_Kim(m_B, x_B, D_eq_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, mug_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, q_B, A_ch_B)
"h_Han_Lee_Kim - chevron angle of 60 degrees"
h_Han_Lee_Kim_B = h_Han_Lee_Kim(m_B, x_B, Dh_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, Cpl_B, q_B, A_ch_B, wavelength_B, chevron_angle_B)
"h_Huang_Sheer - saturation temperature of 283.15 K, contact angle of 35 degrees"
h_Huang_Sheer_B = h_Huang_Sheer(rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, Cpl_B, q_B, Tsat_B, 35)
"h_Yan_Lin"
h_Yan_Lin_B = h_Yan_Lin(m_B, x_B, Dh_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, Cpl_B, q_B, A_ch_B)